  "output": "That check hit a sandboxing quirk (unrelated to the analysis) — not essential, I have enough to go on. Here's the honest finding:\n\n## Reality check on the request\n\nThis repo is **not** a typical application codebase — it's the JARVIS Obsidian vault (markdown notes + metadata). There is no database, no ORM, and no React anywhere in it. So most of your checklist doesn't map:\n\n| Ask | Applies here? |\n|---|---|\n| N+1 queries | No DB/ORM. Closest analog: repeated full-vault directory walks (see below). |\n| React re-renders | No React/frontend code in this repo. |\n| Memory leaks | No long-running processes — everything is a short-lived CLI script that runs once and exits. Nothing to leak. |\n| Caching | Real opportunity — see below. |\n| Redundant computation | Real opportunity — see below. |\n\nThe only real \"code\" is a handful of Node `.mjs` automation scripts under `70 Sistema/Automacao/` (`morning-brief`, `executive-assistant`, `vault-lint`) that read the vault's markdown files and regenerate reports. I reviewed those instead. Two genuine issues:\n\n### 1. `vault-lint/lint.mjs` parses frontmatter 3× per note\n\n`70 Sistema/Automacao/vault-lint/lint.mjs:277-289`:\n\n```js\nfor (const n of notesAtivas) lintFrontmatter(n, erros, avisos);   // parseFrontmatter(n.raw) #1\n...\nlintLinks(notesAtivas, alvosExtras, vaultRoot, erros);\n```\n\n`lintLinks` → `coletarAlvos(notes)` calls `parseFrontmatter(n.raw)` again (`lint.mjs:201`) — parse **#2** — and then `lintLinks`'s own loop (`lint.mjs:234-236`) calls `parseFrontmatter(n.raw)` a **third** time just to split frontmatter from body.\n\nFix: parse once per note up front and pass the parsed object around instead of the raw string.\n\n```js\nconst parsed = notesAtivas.map((n) => ({ ...n, fm: parseFrontmatter(n.raw) }));\nfor (const n of parsed) lintFrontmatter(n, n.fm, erros, avisos);\nlintLinks(parsed, alvosExtras, vaultRoot, erros); // reuse n.fm instead of re-parsing\n```\n\n### 2. `vault-lint` walks the whole tree 4 times\n\n`lint.mjs:271-283`: one walk for the main notes, then separate walks for `90 Arquivo/`, `70 Sistema/Templates/`, and a **fourth walk of the entire vault root again** just to pick up non-`.md` assets (canvas/images/pdf) for link resolution. That's 4 full recursive `fs.readdirSync` passes over the same directory tree in one run.\n\nFix: collect both `.md` and asset extensions in a single walk (the function already accepts an `extensoes` array), instead of re-walking per extension group.\n\n### 3. No cross-script cache for `loadVault()`\n\n`morning-brief/generate.mjs` and `executive-assistant/dashboard.mjs` are independent processes that each call `loadVault(vaultRoot)` — a full synchronous read+parse of every `.md` file in the vault (`70 Sistema/Automacao/morning-brief/lib/vault.mjs:6-18`). If both run close together in the same n8n/cron window (which the RUNBOOKs suggest they do, e.g. morning schedule), the vault gets fully re-read and re-parsed twice for no reason — same files, same content.\n\nIf this becomes a real cost as the vault grows, an mtime-keyed cache (write a `.vault-cache.json` keyed by root-dir mtime, or just check the newest file mtime across the walk) would let the second script reuse the first's parse within the same run. Not worth building unless you've actually noticed the scripts being slow — vault sizes in the hundreds of notes make this microseconds today.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nNothing here rises to \"performance problem\" at current vault scale — these are cron-triggered scripts processing what's likely a few hundred markdown files, finishing in well under a second either way. The frontmatter triple-parse and the 4x directory walk in `vault-lint` are real redundant work and cheap to fix (~10 min), but I wouldn't prioritize them unless `vault-lint` is measurably slow for you. Want me to apply the two fixes above?\n",
